trigger: lessons_completed:17
title: A message from a friend

    A friend sent me a message.

    It says: radiation damages DNA, so any radiation at all is dangerous.

    I want to answer kindly. Can you help me?

reply: that's half right -> chat-mina-worry-half
reply: it's true -> chat-mina-worry-true
reply: what would you say? -> chat-mina-worry-say

node chat-mina-worry-half
kind: reply
pose: proud

    Yes, that's the half my friend has. Ionizing radiation can damage the DNA in a cell.

    What is the other half?

reply: cells repair damage -> chat-mina-worry-repair
reply: it never really matters -> chat-mina-worry-matter

node chat-mina-worry-true
kind: reply
pose: explain

    Part of it is true. Radiation can damage DNA.

    But everyone gets small doses every day and is not ill. So something else must be going on.

reply: repair? -> chat-mina-worry-repair
reply: I'm not sure -> chat-mina-worry-say

node chat-mina-worry-say
kind: reply
pose: explain

    I'd say: yes, radiation can damage DNA. And cells are extremely efficient at repairing that damage.

    So a small everyday dose does not make anyone ill.

reply: that's kind and clear -> chat-mina-worry-end
reply: what if the damage isn't repaired? -> chat-mina-worry-not

node chat-mina-worry-repair
kind: reply
pose: proud

    Yes. Cells are extremely efficient at repairing damage, and they do it all the time.

    That's the missing half.

reply: tell your friend that -> chat-mina-worry-end
reply: what if it isn't repaired? -> chat-mina-worry-not

node chat-mina-worry-matter
kind: reply
pose: think

    It does matter. If damage is not repaired correctly, a cell may die or eventually become cancerous.

    That's why repair, and the size of a dose, matter.

reply: ok, so repair is the other half -> chat-mina-worry-repair
reply: I see -> chat-mina-worry-repair

node chat-mina-worry-not
kind: reply
pose: idle

    Then a cell may die or eventually become cancerous. That's real, and it's why the size of a dose matters.

    A small dose, or one spread over time, gives repair more of a chance.

reply: got it -> chat-mina-worry-end
reply: thanks -> chat-mina-worry-end

node chat-mina-worry-end
kind: sign_off
pose: greet

    I'll send my friend that. Thank you.

    Both halves, said kindly.
